\section*{Curtis stable mod-two Hurewicz image}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Curtis's mod-two stable Hurewicz image is claimed to contain only the span generated by eta, nu, sigma and existing Kervaire classes, with Eccles's assertion following for all positive-dimensional spheres. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 316, 2026-09-25): Iterating suspension until a spherical homology image first disappears leaves a preceding square with a constrained primitive root. Mapping cones turn its detection into Steenrod-module extensions, analyzed through Dickson modules and Cartier halving. Positive terminal levels force Hopf detection; at level zero, weight and Adams-filtration bounds force Kervaire detection, yielding the claimed span.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 316, 2026-09-25): The problem selects the mathematical statement summarized here, including its stated hypotheses and parameter range. The cited theorem gives a claimed proof of that assertion in full.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 316 has no Lean scope page in this snapshot; no formalization of these manuscript results is listed.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

